> problems for what want to do next with the data. So it's clear that the
> dummy variables are estimating on average reasonable values, but something
> in how these values are then rescaled and transformed into a single nominal
> outcome is problematic. My guess is that it has to do with how the omitted
> group is treated and that somehow since it's the residual category people
> it gets assigned last and not often enough.
>
> I've created a moderately simple example that documents this problem. It's
> not the simplest of cases because I've been using panel data, but I'm quite
> confident that the fact that it's panel data is not the cause of the
> problem, especially because I got exactly the same problem with the
> cross-sectional occupation dataset I tried a couple of years ago. Detailed
> documentation of this example, including links to download the actual data,
> are available in this document:
>
> https://dl.dropbox.com/u/16688607/AmeliaProblem/AmeliaFullDocumentation.pdf
>
> Any insights would be greatly appreciated. I may have to resort back to
> Stata's MI program, although I run into mlogit convergence problems there.
>
> Thanks,
